<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	Entitlements for Contents/MacOS/syrinx-app — the Rust binary the bundle's
	launcher execs, and the process every TCC decision is actually made about.
	Applied by scripts/install-macos-dev.sh together with codesign's
	`options runtime` flag; the two only mean anything together, because an
	entitlement here is a *permission to keep* a capability the hardened
	runtime otherwise revokes, not a permission to gain one.

	Mind the prose: a double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment, and
	while plutil lints such a file happily, the parser codesign hands it to
	(AMFIUnserializeXML) rejects it outright with a bare "syntax error near
	line N". Write flag names without their leading dashes, as above.

	NOT sandboxed, deliberately. The app spawns the engine out of a venv (dev)
	or an embedded interpreter (later), reads audio the user points it at
	anywhere on disk, and posts synthetic keystrokes into other apps. Every one
	of those is outside a sandbox by construction, and Syrinx is not headed for
	the Mac App Store.

	This list is short on purpose. An entitlement the binary does not need is a
	hole in the runtime it is signed with, and unlike an Info.plist key a wrong
	one here fails silently and late.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
		The one entitlement this binary genuinely needs, and it covers two
		distinct things:

		1. The microphone. The hardened runtime denies a process any Core Audio
		   *input* stream unless it holds this, and the denial lands before TCC
		   is ever consulted — no prompt, no dialog, just a dead device. That is
		   a separate gate from NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist,
		   which only supplies the prompt's text once TCC does get asked.

		2. The system-audio tap (capture_mac.rs). A CATapDescription tap is
		   wrapped in a private aggregate device, and reading it means running
		   an IOProc over that device's *input* streams — the same HAL gate as
		   the microphone, checked the same way. The tap's own permission
		   (kTCCServiceAudioCapture, prompted for by
		   NSAudioCaptureUsageDescription) sits on top of that, not instead of
		   it: fail the runtime check and the TCC prompt never even fires.

		Note the spelling. `com.apple.security.device.microphone` is the App
		Sandbox key and does nothing under the hardened runtime; this is the
		one codesign's runtime honours.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
		Nothing else, and the omissions are researched rather than accidental:

		* CGEventPost / CGEventCreateKeyboardEvent (dictation_mac.rs) — the
		  hardened runtime does not gate synthetic event posting at all. It is
		  gated by TCC alone (kTCCServicePostEvent, granted through the
		  Accessibility pane), and no com.apple.security.* key exists to ask
		  for it. Denial there is a TCC denial, never a runtime one.

		* Carbon RegisterEventHotKey (the ⌃⌥D chord) — an ordinary Carbon
		  Event Manager registration. No entitlement, and not even a TCC gate:
		  it is a global hotkey, not an event tap.

		* The engine child process. Hardened runtime is per-process and does
		  not propagate across posix_spawn, so the venv's Python inherits none
		  of this — but it *does* inherit TCC responsibility, which is why the
		  mic recording the engine performs is attributed to Syrinx.app and
		  answered by Syrinx's grant.

		* com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation,
		  .allow-unsigned-executable-memory, .allow-jit,
		  .allow-dyld-environment-variables — pure Rust needs none of them, and
		  each one weakens the runtime it is attached to. They belong to the
		  *embedded CPython* of the self-contained bundle phase, on the python
		  binary's own signature: an interpreter signed with the hardened
		  runtime cannot dlopen pip-wheel .so files signed by nobody without
		  disable-library-validation. Put them in a second entitlements file
		  then; do not put them here to save one.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
